..
3.2.3 The "BATCH" Process

.ti 10
The BATCH process is used to start all processes to do
with the Batch System and having done this it quietly exits.
.ti 10
When executed BATCH may ask a question of the operator (ref section 4.3.3)
and then carries out some house keeping chores before starting the following
processes

	* EAR
	* CRD
	* SPL
	* JTD
	* PRINT
	* MAC
	* FTN
	* LIST
	* SHELL
	* MONITOR

.in 10
None of the above listed processes may be started in any other way.

.ig
.ul 1
..
3.2.4 The "EAR" Process

.ti 10
EAR is the process which listens to the ravings of QEI,
monitoring all messages.
Certain messages are detected and cause a change in the Batch system
e.g. when "KRONOS 2.1.2 ..." appears, this is assumed to mean that
EXPORT has bean reinitialized, and so the batch station will login
automatically.

.ig
.ul 1
..
3.2.5 The "CRD" Process

.ti 10
CRD spools cards from the card reader
to be executed locally or sent to the CYBER by the JTD process.
.ti 10
CRD also checks all CYBER jobs for valid job cards.
Invalid jobs are flushed to the local printer
with a message of the form

	"####  illegal job card"

followed by some error message.

.ig
.ul 1
..
3.2.6 The "SPL" Process

.ti 10
SPL spools the printout received from the CYBER to be
printed by the PRINT process.
It may also return the output to the user's directory, if so
specified on the "SUBMIT" command.

.ig
.ul 1
..
3.2.7 The "LPD" Process

.ti 10
LPD is not really part of the Batch system, but is utilized by it.
LPD, the line printer daemon, when started, for example by the LPR command, creates a file
"USR/LPD/LOCK".
if this file already exists, LPD immediately dies.
if not, LPD then examines the directory "/USR/LPD/X" for pointers
to files to be printed and prints them.

.ig
.ul 1
..
3.2.8 The  "PRINT" process

.ti 10
This process takes print jobs from out of the Batch queues and passes
them one by one to "LPD" for printing.

.ig
.ul 1
..

.ig
.ul 1
..
3.2.9 The "MAC" Process

.ti 10
This process takes MACRO jobs from the local input queue,
runs them and places
all output into the local output queue to be printed by PRINT.

.ig
.ul 1
..
3.2.10 The "FTN" Process

.ti 10
This process takes UNIX Fortran jobs from the local input queue,
runs them and places all output into the local output queue to be
printed by PRINT.

.ig
.ul 1
..
3.2.11 The "SHELL" Process

.ti 10
This process takes SHELL jobs from the local input queue,
runs them and places
all output into the local output queue to be printed by PRINT.

.ig
.ul 1
..
3.2.12 The "LIST" Process

.ti 10
This process takes card decks from the local input queue,
lists them and places
all output into the local output queue to be printed by PRINT.

.ig
.ul 1
..
3.2.13 The "JTD" Process

.ti 10
JTD, the cyber daemon,
when created examines the local input queues for spooled
card decks to be sent to the CYBER and sends them.
After sending all available decks JTD goes to sleep until
more CYBER jobs enter the QUEUES.

.ig
.ul 1
..
3.2.14 The "JA" Process

.ti 10
JA is a process run to analyse stored statistical information.
The information is stored by the Batch System software.

.ig
.ul 1
..
3.2.15 The 200-UT Driver

.ti 10
The 200-UT driver is the software interface between the CYBER
line and the above batch station processes.
It has been extensively rewritten and reduced in size for
this new release of the Batch System.
This interface is implemented as a set of four device files:

	* CYBER commands up (cyb.cmdsu)
	* CYBER commands down (cyb.cmdsd)
	* CYBER jobs up (cyb.jobsu)
	* CYBER jobs down. (cyb.jobsd)

.ig
.ul 1
..
3.2.16 The Card Reader Driver

.ti 10
The card reader driver is the software responsible
for servicing the card reader interrupt.
Card data is transfered via the device file "/DEV/CR0".

.ig
.ul 1
..
3.2.17 The Printer Driver

.ti 10
The printer driver is the software responsible for servicing the printer
interrupt.
This driver has been rewritten to use the internal buffer pool
so that data is not logically
passed to it character by character but in blocks 512 bytes long.
The printer is driven at the same speeds but with less load on the CPU.
Print data is transfered via a device file "/DEV/LP".

